<header><p class="st-kicker">theocracy · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Hospitaller Malta</h1><p class="st-meta">1530 CE – 1798 CE · capital Valletta</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The crusading Order of Saint John's island state, which defeated the Ottoman Great Siege of 1565 and built Valletta.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Expelled from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/rhodes">Rhodes</a> by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> in 1522, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-hospitaller">Knights Hospitaller</a> received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malta">Malta</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gozo">Gozo</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-tripoli">Tripoli</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Emperor Charles V</a> in 1530, in return for a symbolic annual falcon. Grand Master <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippe-villiers-de-lisle-adam">Philippe Villiers de L'Isle-Adam</a> settled the Order at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/birgu">Birgu</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Order, a military-religious brotherhood of European nobles divided into national langues, was ruled by an elected Grand Master. In 1565 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-parisot-de-valette">Jean de Valette</a> defended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malta">Malta</a> against the Ottoman Great Siege, and the new capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valletta">Valletta</a> was built from 1566. The knights waged corsair war on Muslim shipping, ran a celebrated hospital, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sacra-infermeria">Sacra Infermeria</a>, and built fortifications, churches and palaces.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1798 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon's</a> fleet, on its way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, demanded entry; Grand Master <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-von-hompesch-zu-bolheim">Ferdinand von Hompesch</a> surrendered without a fight. The French were expelled in 1800, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malta">Malta</a> became a British possession. The Order survives as a sovereign body in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/hospitaller-malta.jpg" alt="Hospitaller Malta" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Zscout370 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Order_of_St._John_(various).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1530 CE – 1798 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>theocracy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Valletta</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Philippe Villiers de L'Isle-Adam, Jean de Valette, Manuel Pinto da Fonseca</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Maltese, Italian, French, Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/malta">Malta</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Order of Saint John in Malta, Knights of Malta (state)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1664&amp;focus=hospitaller-malta" aria-label="Hospitaller Malta on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5046 881 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Hospitaller Malta lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5366 1137L5338 1239M5366 1137L5333 1064M5366 1137L5054 728M5366 1137L3295 1791"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5338 1239h0M5333 1064h0M5054 728h0M3295 1791h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5366 1137h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5366 1137h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Valletta. <a href="/atlas?year=1664&amp;focus=hospitaller-malta">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

